# shellcheck shell=bash
#
# shared/guard.lib -- what a guard's exit status says, for both commit-time
# gates (issue 0720). SOURCED, never run: it is 0644 so `is_handler` (-f AND -x)
# cannot mistake it for a command.
#
# THE ONE READING OF A GUARD'S EXIT STATUS (issue 0714). A guard whose status is
# read runs as `x=0; bash <tool> || x=$?`, never as a bare call: under `set -e`
# a bare call's non-zero exit ends the gate where it stands, before it reports,
# so the commit gate never reaches its later guards or its summary, and a cut
# gate never names the log and the remedy. Its status then means one thing for
# every guard of a kind, as guard_verdict below sets out, and no verdict is
# named by its code and never by a cause it did not establish, since a control
# that did not fire, a tool's own die() and bash's 126 and 127 all land there.
# A gating guard's 1 is its finding only as far as its tool keeps 1 for one:
# bash's own `set -u` abort ends a script with 1, a command failing under `-e`
# can, and either reads as that guard's finding.
#
# BOTH GATES READ A GUARD'S STATUS HERE, AND EACH REPORTS IT ITS OWN WAY. The
# commit gate (cmd/precommit) adds the guard's line to its summary and runs the
# guards after it. A cut gate (cut/gate) refuses at once, naming the log the
# guard wrote and its remedy. Until issue 0720 the cut's contract and reference
# checks each restated this reading in their own `case` arms and their own
# words ("gave no verdict (rc=N)"), so a change to what a status means could
# reach one gate and not the other.
#
# A GUARD'S KIND IS NAMED BY ITS CALLER, NEVER IMPLIED BY A BRANCH (issue 0724).
# A report-only guard prints its findings and exits 0 with them, since they
# never gate, so it gives no status a finding's meaning. A kind nobody declared
# is refused, since reading its guard as either kind would claim a meaning its
# caller never gave.

# guard_verdict <status> <kind> -- what the exit <status> of a guard of <kind>,
# `gating` or `report-only`, says, on stdout: `pass` for 0, `finding` for a
# gating guard's 1, and for any other status the one wording of no verdict,
# `gave no verdict (exit <status>)`, which its caller follows with where the
# guard's own output is. A caller reads `pass` and `finding` by name and takes
# anything else as no verdict, so a status this does not know can never read as
# a pass. A <kind> it does not know is refused on stderr, with status 2.
guard_verdict() {
  case "${2:-}:$1" in
    gating:0 | report-only:0) printf 'pass\n' ;;
    gating:1) printf 'finding\n' ;;
    gating:* | report-only:*) printf 'gave no verdict (exit %s)\n' "$1" ;;
    *)
      printf 'guard_verdict: no kind of guard is named "%s"; a guard is gating or report-only\n' "${2:-}" >&2
      return 2
      ;;
  esac
}
